1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
A passenger jet is
crossing the Indian Ocean.

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:05,100
A commuter train approaches a station.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,880
Far below, a child is
walking along a beach.

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:14,420
Nothing looks unusual. Yet
every one of them is moving

5
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:18,260
east with the turning earth,
even when they appear perfectly

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,620
still. At the equator,
that motion is about 1

7
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,720
,600 and 70 kilometers per hour.

8
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:30,760
Now imagine that. In a
single, impossible instant,

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,120
the solid earth stops rotating.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,020
The planet does not stop orbiting the sun.

11
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:39,460
Gravity does not disappear.

12
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:43,360
And this is not something
nature can simply do without an

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,760
extraordinary force. We are
isolating one terrible change

14
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,640
to see what physics predicts.

15
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:06,020
The ground halts. But
the air, the oceans, and

16
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:09,460
objects that were moving
with it still have momentum.

17
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:14,760
For a fraction of a second,
everything seems almost normal.

18
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,080
Then the world around
you tries to keep going.

19
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,820
And the direction of disaster is east.

20
00:01:25,138 --> 00:01:26,978
You are standing on a city pavement.

21
00:01:28,038 --> 00:01:30,898
The pavement suddenly
loses its eastward motion.

22
00:01:31,798 --> 00:01:36,198
Your body does not. In this
thought experiment, you

23
00:01:36,198 --> 00:01:40,698
keep the speed you had just
before the stop, relative to

24
00:01:40,698 --> 00:01:45,118
the halted ground. Near
the equator, that is faster

25
00:01:45,118 --> 00:01:47,538
than many passenger aircraft fly.

26
00:01:48,338 --> 00:01:50,258
You would not be launched into space.

27
00:01:51,698 --> 00:01:54,558
Earth's gravity is still
far too strong for that.

28
00:01:55,138 --> 00:01:58,658
But being hurled across the
surface at hundreds of meters per

29
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:00,478
second would be catastrophic.

30
00:02:01,938 --> 00:02:03,898
Vehicles slide and collide.

31
00:02:04,858 --> 00:02:06,458
Loose equipment tears free.

32
00:02:07,198 --> 00:02:10,338
Trees and structures encounter
enormous sideways forces.

33
00:02:11,478 --> 00:02:15,498
Near the equator, almost nothing
exposed would remain safe.

34
00:02:16,718 --> 00:02:18,638
The speed is different across the planet.

35
00:02:19,378 --> 00:02:22,118
The farther you are from the
equator, the smaller your

36
00:02:22,118 --> 00:02:26,118
eastward speed. At the exact
geographic poles it approaches

37
00:02:26,118 --> 00:02:30,318
zero. That does not make the
polar regions comfortable havens.

38
00:02:31,218 --> 00:02:33,878
It only means their initial
jolt would be less severe.

39
00:02:35,098 --> 00:02:36,998
And this is just the motion of objects.

40
00:02:37,898 --> 00:02:39,158
The atmosphere is next.

41
00:02:41,942 --> 00:02:46,502
From above the clouds, Earth
seems unchanged, but the

42
00:02:46,502 --> 00:02:50,522
surface below has stopped, while
an entire moving atmosphere

43
00:02:50,522 --> 00:02:59,972
carries on. Near the

44
00:02:59,972 --> 00:03:03,252
equator, air initially has
an eastward speed of roughly

45
00:03:03,252 --> 00:03:07,812
1670 kmh relative to the newly stationary

46
00:03:07,812 --> 00:03:12,212
ground. That is beyond
the speed of sound under

47
00:03:12,212 --> 00:03:16,292
ordinary conditions. Do not
picture a normal hurricane.

48
00:03:16,872 --> 00:03:18,012
Simply turned up to 11.

49
00:03:18,012 --> 00:03:21,612
This would be an unprecedented
global flow, colliding with

50
00:03:21,612 --> 00:03:25,372
mountains, forests, buildings, and terrain.

51
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:30,272
Turbulence, pressure changes,
and friction would make the

52
00:03:30,272 --> 00:03:34,032
details far more complicated
than any simple number.

53
00:03:37,372 --> 00:03:39,572
Dust rises in vast walls.

54
00:03:41,172 --> 00:03:44,352
Windows shatter. Roofs peel away.

55
00:03:45,672 --> 00:03:47,832
Cities disappear beneath flying debris.

56
00:03:47,832 --> 00:03:59,472
The strongest initial effect

57
00:03:59,472 --> 00:04:01,232
would be in lower latitudes.

58
00:04:02,212 --> 00:04:06,152
Closer to the poles, the
rotational speed is smaller.

59
00:04:07,512 --> 00:04:12,072
But the atmosphere does not
respect borders or neat rings on

60
00:04:12,072 --> 00:04:16,112
a map. The air would lose
energy as it collided with the

61
00:04:16,112 --> 00:04:19,592
landscape, generating heat and destruction.

62
00:04:20,472 --> 00:04:24,692
And beneath that air,
another moving layer covers

63
00:04:24,692 --> 00:04:26,112
most of the planet.

64
00:04:27,392 --> 00:04:27,932
Water.

65
00:04:30,541 --> 00:04:32,801
A cargo ship is crossing calm water.

66
00:04:33,901 --> 00:04:35,081
Then its world changes.

67
00:04:36,401 --> 00:04:39,421
The solid seafloor has
lost its rotational speed.

68
00:04:40,481 --> 00:04:42,241
The ocean above it has not.

69
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:47,641
Momentum drives immense flows
across basins toward coastlines

70
00:04:47,641 --> 00:04:50,021
and against underwater terrain.

71
00:04:51,121 --> 00:04:55,541
Near some shores, the result
could be catastrophic inundation.

72
00:04:56,061 --> 00:04:59,941
In others, the details would
depend on coastline shape,

73
00:05:00,621 --> 00:05:04,901
sea bed depth, local
geography, and exactly how the

74
00:05:04,901 --> 00:05:06,721
fictional stopping force acted.

75
00:05:07,961 --> 00:05:11,181
This would not be one perfect
wall of water circling the

76
00:05:11,181 --> 00:05:15,701
planet. Real water
breaks, reflects, swirls,

77
00:05:15,861 --> 00:05:17,201
and collides with itself.

78
00:05:18,241 --> 00:05:22,641
Imagine a harbor swept by surging
water and smashed shipping

79
00:05:22,641 --> 00:05:27,001
containers. An airport
runway vanishing below foam.

80
00:05:28,141 --> 00:05:31,301
Bridges overwhelmed by
currents carrying wreckage.

81
00:05:32,621 --> 00:05:36,381
As the initial motion dissipates,
waves and flooding could

82
00:05:36,381 --> 00:05:38,601
continue to transform coastlines.

83
00:05:39,621 --> 00:05:43,421
There is another problem waiting
far beyond the first surge.

84
00:05:44,461 --> 00:05:48,541
Earth's rotation helps shape
its oceans and equatorial bulge.

85
00:05:48,941 --> 00:05:53,101
Without that rotation, water
would tend to redistribute over

86
00:05:53,101 --> 00:05:58,381
much longer timescales. The maps we
know would no longer be reliable.

87
00:06:01,069 --> 00:06:05,129
Now step back from the spectacle,
imagine the morning after,

88
00:06:05,609 --> 00:06:09,649
wherever anyone remains. A
power station has been damaged,

89
00:06:10,089 --> 00:06:14,529
transmission lines are down,
water pumps are silent, roads

90
00:06:14,529 --> 00:06:18,669
are blocked by collapsed
structures and debris, emergency

91
00:06:18,669 --> 00:06:22,229
services have lost the very
networks they need to coordinate.

92
00:06:23,149 --> 00:06:26,849
Not every building would fail,
and not every region would

93
00:06:26,849 --> 00:06:30,789
experience the same damage,
but modern life depends on

94
00:06:30,789 --> 00:06:32,889
thousands of systems working together.

95
00:06:33,709 --> 00:06:37,629
Electricity keeps hospitals
functioning, fuel deliveries keep

96
00:06:37,629 --> 00:06:41,389
generators running,
communications connect rescuers,

97
00:06:41,969 --> 00:06:45,149
food travels through ports,
warehouses and roads.

98
00:06:46,249 --> 00:06:49,949
Break enough links at once, and
even a place that escaped the

99
00:06:49,949 --> 00:06:52,129
worst winds may face a second disaster.

100
00:06:53,169 --> 00:06:56,509
You might have survived the
initial blast inside a tunnel or

101
00:06:56,509 --> 00:07:00,389
strong underground shelter.
You could emerge into a world

102
00:07:00,389 --> 00:07:03,889
where clean water is harder to
find than a collapsed building.

103
00:07:05,089 --> 00:07:09,569
Our civilization can withstand
storms, earthquakes and floods,

104
00:07:10,409 --> 00:07:13,709
but never all of them, almost everywhere,

105
00:07:14,569 --> 00:07:15,949
in the same moment.

106
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:22,697
Could anyone survive? The
honest answer is that we

107
00:07:22,697 --> 00:07:25,977
cannot calculate an exact
number from this impossible

108
00:07:25,977 --> 00:07:30,237
scenario, but some locations
would offer better odds

109
00:07:30,237 --> 00:07:34,217
than others. Deep underground
shelters could shield people

110
00:07:34,217 --> 00:07:35,997
from the initial wind and debris.

111
00:07:36,857 --> 00:07:40,917
Certain polar locations would
experience far less immediate

112
00:07:40,917 --> 00:07:45,217
eastward momentum. Some
isolated places might

113
00:07:45,217 --> 00:07:48,397
retain usable water, fuel, or food.

114
00:07:49,437 --> 00:07:52,557
Yet, a lucky location is not enough.

115
00:07:53,537 --> 00:07:57,717
Survival would depend
on air, warmth, medical

116
00:07:57,717 --> 00:08:01,897
care, reliable shelter, and
a way to produce supplies

117
00:08:01,897 --> 00:08:04,617
after the global transport
network collapsed.

118
00:08:05,877 --> 00:08:09,757
Think of a small research
station or a group inside a

119
00:08:09,757 --> 00:08:13,487
reinforced underground facility.
They might live through the

120
00:08:13,487 --> 00:08:17,987
first minutes. Days later,
every choice becomes difficult.

121
00:08:19,107 --> 00:08:20,427
Do they risk traveling outside?

122
00:08:21,687 --> 00:08:23,327
Can they repair their power supply?

123
00:08:24,447 --> 00:08:27,027
What grows when the
sunlight pattern changes?

124
00:08:27,727 --> 00:08:31,427
We have been describing the
violence of a single second, but

125
00:08:31,427 --> 00:08:34,667
the most profound change may be slower.

126
00:08:34,667 --> 00:08:39,127
It begins when someone looks
at a clock and realizes that

127
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:42,407
mourning is not behaving
like mourning anymore.

128
00:08:45,145 --> 00:08:49,245
Our ordinary day exists
because Earth rotates

129
00:08:49,245 --> 00:08:53,725
beneath the sun. Take
that rotation away, but

130
00:08:53,725 --> 00:08:57,585
let Earth continue its yearly
orbit, and the sun's position in

131
00:08:57,585 --> 00:09:01,105
the sky would change only slowly
as the planet moves around

132
00:09:01,105 --> 00:09:05,185
its star. Instead of a
sunrise every 24 hours,

133
00:09:05,725 --> 00:09:10,025
many places would experience
extraordinarily long intervals of

134
00:09:10,025 --> 00:09:14,305
light and darkness. The
precise pattern would vary with

135
00:09:14,305 --> 00:09:17,865
latitude and season because
Earth's axis is tilted.

136
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:22,285
You would not get one
permanently bright side and one

137
00:09:22,285 --> 00:09:26,285
permanently dark side. That
would require the planet to keep

138
00:09:26,285 --> 00:09:30,065
exactly the same face toward
the sun as it orbited.

139
00:09:30,785 --> 00:09:35,185
Under our scenario, the sun
could linger above the horizon

140
00:09:35,185 --> 00:09:39,425
for months, followed by prolonged darkness.

141
00:09:40,375 --> 00:09:44,685
Daylit regions would absorb heat
for far longer than before.

142
00:09:45,725 --> 00:09:47,005
Dark regions would cool.

143
00:09:48,065 --> 00:09:52,505
Agriculture, ecosystems, and
the rhythms of daily life would

144
00:09:52,505 --> 00:09:55,345
face conditions they had
never evolved to handle.

145
00:09:56,685 --> 00:10:00,265
But the absence of ordinary
days is only the beginning.

146
00:10:00,265 --> 00:10:05,465
The winds themselves would eventually
learn a different pattern.

147
00:10:07,767 --> 00:10:12,267
Today, Earth's rotation
helps steer winds and ocean

148
00:10:12,267 --> 00:10:15,307
currents through the Coriolis effect.

149
00:10:16,247 --> 00:10:19,407
It contributes to the patterns
that form familiar weather

150
00:10:19,407 --> 00:10:23,747
systems. Without that rotation, the old

151
00:10:23,747 --> 00:10:26,467
circulation could not
simply continue forever.

152
00:10:27,487 --> 00:10:31,327
As the first chaos faded, the
planet would reorganize around

153
00:10:31,327 --> 00:10:35,707
slow, solar heating, enormous temperature

154
00:10:35,707 --> 00:10:39,367
contrasts, and a changing
distribution of water.

155
00:10:40,607 --> 00:10:44,467
We cannot draw an exact weather
map for such an Earth from one

156
00:10:44,467 --> 00:10:48,067
thought experiment. The
details would require enormous

157
00:10:48,067 --> 00:10:52,467
simulations and assumptions.
But we can say that the

158
00:10:52,467 --> 00:10:55,647
planet's climate would not
resemble the world outside your

159
00:10:55,647 --> 00:11:00,167
window today. And one popular
claim needs correcting.

160
00:11:01,247 --> 00:11:04,767
Stopping the surface rotation
does not mean Earth's magnetic

161
00:11:04,767 --> 00:11:07,807
field automatically switches
off at that instant.

162
00:11:08,867 --> 00:11:12,627
The magnetic field is generated
by complex motion inside the

163
00:11:12,627 --> 00:11:17,127
liquid outer core, not a light
switch connected to the 24-hour

164
00:11:17,127 --> 00:11:21,647
day. The real disaster is
already vast enough without

165
00:11:21,647 --> 00:11:26,147
inventing another one. Earth would still

166
00:11:26,147 --> 00:11:30,547
be a planet. The question
is whether it would

167
00:11:30,547 --> 00:11:33,787
remain a planet that humans could inhabit.

168
00:11:36,392 --> 00:11:38,532
Imagine the closing image.

169
00:11:39,752 --> 00:11:42,632
The camera rises above a damaged coastline.

170
00:11:43,712 --> 00:11:45,532
Roads vanish beneath sediment.

171
00:11:46,752 --> 00:11:50,952
An empty city lies under a
sky that refuses to hurry

172
00:11:50,952 --> 00:11:55,412
towards sunset. It is tempting to ask where

173
00:11:55,412 --> 00:11:57,012
the safest place would be.

174
00:11:58,372 --> 00:12:00,432
The poles? A mountain bunker?

175
00:12:01,492 --> 00:12:03,032
A sealed research facility?

176
00:12:03,032 --> 00:12:07,392
But the better question is, what stopped

177
00:12:07,392 --> 00:12:11,432
and what was allowed to keep moving?

178
00:12:12,512 --> 00:12:16,392
If the atmosphere and oceans
somehow stopped with the crust,

179
00:12:16,852 --> 00:12:20,672
the first seconds would
unfold very differently.

180
00:12:21,712 --> 00:12:26,012
If the planet slowed over millions
of years, there would be no

181
00:12:26,012 --> 00:12:28,252
single day of global impact.

182
00:12:28,252 --> 00:12:32,132
And if a real force
removed Earth's rotation

183
00:12:32,132 --> 00:12:36,312
in an instant, dealing with the energy and

184
00:12:36,312 --> 00:12:40,812
angular momentum would create
problems beyond anything in our

185
00:12:40,812 --> 00:12:44,472
simplified story. This is not a prediction.

186
00:12:45,572 --> 00:12:49,612
There is no known natural process
waiting to switch our planet

187
00:12:49,612 --> 00:12:54,152
off tomorrow. It is a reminder of something

188
00:12:54,152 --> 00:12:58,572
stranger. The ground feels still because

189
00:12:58,572 --> 00:12:59,852
we are moving with it.

190
00:13:01,452 --> 00:13:05,772
The calmest moment of your
day is taking place on a world

191
00:13:05,772 --> 00:13:07,872
that never stops turning.

192
00:13:08,932 --> 00:13:13,192
Until, in our imagination, it

193
00:13:13,192 --> 00:13:17,052
does. And then even standing

194
00:13:17,052 --> 00:13:19,732
still becomes impossible.

